# The two `Exception` classes must stay separately nameable from a .npy:
#
#   Exception          -> the PYTHON root (pylib's)
#   su.Exception       -> sysutils' Pascal class, and only it
#   "Exception"        -> a string, untouched
#   class E(Exception) -> the Python root, with no special case
#
# HOW that is achieved changed, and the assertions below deliberately did not.
# Under decide-pylib-exception-vs-sysutils-exception option 5 the separation came
# from a pylexer RENAME (bare `Exception` -> `PyException`). That mechanism is
# GONE: it mapped the name on the way in and never on the way out, so `repr()`
# and `type(e).__name__` reported the internal spelling
# (bug-nilpy-exception-repr-and-type-name-say-pyexception).
#
# Since variant C (feature-a-one-exception-class-in-a-shared-unit) the two are
# genuine SIBLING classes named `Exception`, under a shared `ExceptionBase`, each
# exported by its own unit — so they are told apart by which unit you name, which
# is the ordinary rule rather than a special case. This file is the regression
# test that the observable contract survived swapping the mechanism underneath
# it: every line below asserts exactly what it did before.
# `'sysutils.pas'`, quoted: a bare NilPy import means PYTHON, and sysutils is a
# genuinely Pascal unit, so it is reached by naming its extension. Unchanged in
# every other respect -- the alias still keys on the basename.
import 'sysutils.pas' as su


class MyErr(Exception):
    pass


# the bare name is the Python root: it catches a Python raise...
try:
    raise MyErr("mine")
except Exception as e:
    print("bare caught:", e)

# ...and BRIDGES to the Pascal root as well, because in Python `except
# Exception:` catches everything the program can raise and an RTL raise is
# still that. The bridge is an explicit catch rule in pyparser's except arm,
# not a namespace merge.
try:
    x = su.StrToInt("abc")
except Exception as e:
    print("bridge caught:", e)

# the QUALIFIED name is the Pascal class, and only it. `e` is a Pascal object
# here, so its MESSAGE is read through the Pascal property — print(e) on it
# gives Python's generic object repr, which is honest: CPython has no such
# class, so nothing about upward compatibility says otherwise.
try:
    x = su.StrToInt("nope")
except su.Exception as e:
    print("qualified caught:", e.Message)

# a string literal spelling it is untouched
print("literal:", "Exception")
print("end")
